   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Weekly Malaria Burden Forecasting and Uncertainty Quantification\n",
    "\n",
    "## Project motivation\n",
    "\n",
    "This project began with a practical question: **could routinely collected malaria surveillance data eventually support short-term preparedness at Howard Mission Hospital in Zimbabwe?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Sufficiently detailed local historical malaria data were not readily accessible during development, so an available longitudinal malaria surveillance dataset from Peru was used as a **proof of concept**. The goal was to build and stress-test the forecasting workflow first: weekly aggregation, epidemiological-week alignment, weather integration, lagged predictors, count regression, machine-learning comparison, peak diagnostics, high-burden classification, and uncertainty quantification.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Important:** the fitted Peru model is **not** intended for direct operational use at Howard Mission Hospital. What is transferable is the modelling framework. A Howard-specific version would require local weekly surveillance data, retraining, a locally defined high-burden threshold, and local out-of-sample validation.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Reproducibility note\n",
    "\n",
    "The raw surveillance and weather files are not included in the public repository. To rerun the notebook locally, place the source files in the repository's `data/` folder using the filenames referenced below.\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook keeps the exploratory reasoning visible, including models that did not improve forecasting. That is intentional: the aim is to show how the final modelling choices were reached rather than present only the successful result.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
