  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 29,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "weekly_weather_clean = weekly_weather_clean.rename(\n",
    "    columns={\n",
    "        \"epi_year\": \"year\",\n",
    "        \"epi_week\": \"epidermiological week\"\n",
    "    }\n",
    ")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Why rebuild the weeks?\n",
    "\n",
    "The malaria data are indexed by epidemiological week, while the weather source is hourly. If the two sources use different week boundaries, rainfall from one period could be incorrectly paired with malaria cases from another. The hourly timestamps are therefore shifted to Peru local time, grouped into Sunday-Saturday weeks, and converted to epidemiological year/week before merging.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Merge malaria and weather data, then audit the merge\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 30,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "model_data = weekly_cases.merge(\n",
    "    weekly_weather_clean[\n",
    "        [\n",
    "            \"year\",\n",
    "            \"epidermiological week\",\n",
    "            \"avg_temperature\",\n",
    "            \"total_rainfall\"\n",
    "        ]\n",
    "    ],\n",
    "    on=[\"year\", \"epidermiological week\"],\n",
    "    how=\"inner\",\n",
    "    validate=\"one_to_one\"\n",
    ")"
   ]
  },
