   "source": [
    "### Why uncertainty was added\n",
    "\n",
    "A point forecast such as “170 cases” can look much more certain than the model really is. Malaria counts vary even when the predictors look similar, and the fitted Negative Binomial model explicitly represents that extra variability.\n",
    "\n",
    "The predictive interval therefore asks a different question from the point estimate: **what range of future case counts does the fitted model consider reasonably plausible?**\n",
    "\n",
    "For an 80% predictive interval, the lower and upper limits are the 10th and 90th percentiles of the model's predictive distribution. In the 2023-2024 test period, the nominal 80% interval contained 96 of 104 observed weeks (92.3%). That means the intervals were conservative: they covered more often than the nominal 80% level would suggest.\n",
    "\n",
    "Among the 12 high-burden weeks, 9 were inside the interval. Three major surges exceeded even the upper predictive bound, showing genuine forecast failures rather than merely imperfect point estimates.\n",
    "\n",
    "The model-implied probability of exceeding 206 cases showed useful ranking signal (ROC AUC about 0.79), but it was not strongly calibrated enough to treat as a clinically validated alert probability. It is best interpreted as a **model-estimated high-burden risk score**.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 18. Final interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "The simplified Negative Binomial model was retained as the core forecasting model because it was competitive, interpretable, and simpler than the alternatives tested. It modestly improved on persistence and quantified uncertainty reasonably well, but it did **not** reliably anticipate abrupt malaria surges.\n",
    "\n",
    "The project therefore supports an **uncertainty-aware forecasting feasibility framework**, not a claim of a deployment-ready early-warning system.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Planned Howard Mission Hospital adaptation\n",
    "\n",
    "If local aggregated surveillance data become available, the next stage would be to:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. rebuild weekly case counts from Howard data;\n",
    "2. define a Howard-specific high-burden threshold;\n",
    "3. reconstruct local seasonality;\n",
    "4. obtain locally representative weather data if useful;\n",
    "5. retrain all model parameters from scratch;\n",
    "6. validate on later Howard observations; and\n",
    "7. expose the final output through a simple hospital-facing interface rather than requiring staff to run Python.\n",
    "\n",
    "The Peru-fitted coefficients and thresholds should not be directly transferred to Zimbabwe.\n"
   ]
